(project pricklypear-single-image
 (doc "Book chapter: the single-image pivot. Supersedes the
       supervisor + per-user-image + authd topology described in
       architecture.borg, supervisor.borg, and multi-user.borg.
       One process. One binary. One deployment. Multi-user at the
       session/branch layer (not process isolation).")
 (status implemented)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section single-image
  (doc "The pivot: one process, single-tenant, no Mirage abstraction")
  (status implemented)
  
  (subsection thesis
   (doc "What the system IS after the pivot")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      Pricklypear is ONE OCaml `unix' binary — the image — that:
        1. Serves htmx HTTP directly on a port (httpaf-lwt-unix
           + pure-html). This is the only user-facing surface.
        2. Talks to Postgres directly (pgx_lwt + Io_unix).
           Postgres is the durable object store; the image is
           ephemeral and rebuildable from it.
        3. Reads its identity (branch, author) from process env
           at boot — no boot manifest, no supervisor injection.
        4. Optionally exposes a line-protocol REPL on a second
           port for operator access. No SSH front door. The
           browser REPL (/repl) and HTTP eval (/api/eval) are
           the primary interactive surfaces.

      What the system is NOT, after the pivot:
        - NOT a MirageOS unikernel. No Solo5 target. No
          mirage/unix target. No hypervisor VM-request spawn.
          `dune build` + restart is how you change the image.
        - NOT multi-user at the process level. One tenant.
          The branch model (fork/merge) stays for
          experimentation, but there is no per-user image
          instance, no fleet, no per-user isolation.
        - NOT fronted by a supervisor. No authd sidecar.
          The image is the only Pricklypear process.

      This is a deliberate simplification. The unikervisor
      model was sound in theory but every deployment pain in
      it1-it34 (EADDRINUSE on respawn, stale route tables,
      orphaned image PIDs, the awa-ssh identity block, manual
      spawn-vs-supervised-spawn confusion) traces back to
      having three coordinated processes. One process cannot
      have those failures.
    |)))
  )
  (subsection removed
   (doc "What is deleted from the codebase and spec")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      Packages deleted:
        - supervisor/        (fleet, health, process, ssh,
                             ssh_awa, ssh_awa_shims,
                             ssh_keys, auth_client, admin,
                             boot, store) — ALL of it.
        - auth/              (verify, tokens, admin_api,
                             store, http, boot, authd.ml) —
                             ALL of it as a SEPARATE process.
                             The users table stays; the
                             verify logic (password/pubkey)
                             either folds into the image as a
                             thin prim or is replaced by
                             nginx basic-auth at the edge.

      Spec chapters demoted (status -> dropped, cross-link
      here as the successor):
        - supervisor.borg     whole chapter: dropped
        - multi-user.borg     isolation by process: dropped;
                              namespaces/budgets/permissions
                              stay as Lisp-level safeguards
                              within the one image
        - architecture.borg   topology + transport-choices +
                              package-layout: rewritten by this
                              chapter

      Migrations trimmed:
        - 002_auth.sql: keep `users'; drop `credentials',
          `auth_sessions', `auth_log', `system_keys' UNLESS
          the image does its own verify (then keep only what
          the folded verify reads). Additive-only — no
          destructive migration; orphaned tables are inert.

      rc.d services collapsed:
        - pricklypear_supervisor, pricklypear_authd  ->  DELETE
        - pricklypear (single image)                  ->  ADD
        One service. One binary. nginx fronts it.

      scripts/dev.sh simplified:
        - start  -> PG + image (two things, not three)
        - stop/restart/status -> the image only
    |)))
  )
  (subsection kept
   (doc "What survives the pivot unchanged in shape")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      Untouched:
        - lisp/            pure interpreter; no I/O dep
        - common/          manifest.ml, branch.ml (trim
                           auth_types.ml, protocol.ml health)
        - image/lib/       store, primitives, session, http,
                           crud, repl_endpoint, lineedit
        - libs/*.pp        all Lisp libraries (std, ui, ai,
                           todo, bible, dabar, notes, outline)
        - migrations/      001_branches, 003_data, 004_bible
                           (002_auth trimmed, not dropped)
        - scripts/seed_*   bible/strongs/interlinear seeds

      Refactored (not deleted):
        - image/lib/boot.ml   reads PP_USERNAME/PP_BRANCH_NAME/
                              PP_BRANCH_ID from env directly;
                              drops the supervisor-manifest path
        - image/bin/unikernel.ml  renamed image/bin/main.ml;
                                  plain `unix' entry, no mirage
        - image/lib/dune      `(executable ... public_name image)';
                              drop mirage/unix stanza
        - dune-project        drop mirage/unix enabled flag
    |)))
  )
  (subsection topology
   (doc "The new physical layout")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      OpenBSD host (build + runtime habitat):
        +-- Postgres (shared object store, SERIALIZABLE
        |              defaults)
        |
        +-- pricklypear image (ONE `unix' binary)
        |   +-- httpaf-lwt-unix on PP_HTTP_PORT (htmx UI,
        |   |   /api/eval, /repl, /dabar, /todos, /notes)
        |   +-- optional line-protocol REPL on
        |   |   PP_REPL_PORT (operator only)
        |   +-- pgx_lwt to Postgres (functions, data_rows,
        |       branches)
        |
        +-- nginx (edge)
            +-- TLS / basic-auth -> proxy_pass
                http://127.0.0.1:PP_HTTP_PORT

      Flow for "user adds a function":
        1. Open the browser (nginx front door).
        2. /repl or /edit/<name>: define persists to
           function_defs, registered live.
        3. New htmx route serves it immediately.

      Flow for "operator changes a primitive":
        1. Edit image/lib/primitives.ml.
        2. `dune build' + git push (post-receive rebuilds +
           promotes the release).
        3. `rcctl restart pricklypear' — one process, one
           restart. No fleet, no A/B, no respawn race.

      Flow for "operator deploys":
        1. git push wyo master.
        2. post-receive: dune build -> promote to
           /usr/local/libexec/pricklypear/releases/<sha> ->
           `current' symlink.
        3. rcctl restart pricklypear.
    |)))
  )
  (subsection auth
   (doc "Identity without requiring authd as a second always-on binary")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      MULTI-USER is required (many humans, personal gardens).
      See auth.multi-user-product for the complete user stories.

      This pivot only answers: where does verify run?

      Option A — edge gate only (insufficient as product):
        nginx basic-auth / htpasswd. NOT the multi-user user
        database. Acceptable only as temporary break-glass.

      Option B — in-image verify (fits one binary):
        Password/session verify in the image process against
        users + credentials + auth_sessions. /login cookie
        middleware. No separate authd required for web login.
        Still multi-user: many sessions, many personal branches.

      Option C — authd process (fits fleet multi-user):
        Separate verify service as originally designed; supervisor
        attaches per-user images. Complete isolation story.

      Complete system: multi-user product stories + either B or C
      for verify placement + multi-user.isolation for process
      isolation when fleet is live.
    |)))
  )
  (subsection deployment
   (doc "One binary, one rc.d service, one restart")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      rc.d service `pricklypear':
        /etc/rc.d/pricklypear
          start -> /usr/local/libexec/pricklypear/current/bin/image
                   with env from /etc/pricklypear/pricklypear.env
          stop  -> kill the single PID
          restart -> stop + start (no EADDRINUSE because
                    one process, one port, clean shutdown)

      post-receive hook (existing, simplified):
        1. dune build @all (lisp + common + image only;
           no supervisor, no auth)
        2. promote to releases/<sha>
        3. swap `current' symlink
        4. rcctl restart pricklypear

      nginx (existing, simplified):
        - one server block: auth_basic + proxy_pass to
          127.0.0.1:PP_HTTP_PORT
        - drop the /admin -> 17880 block (supervisor gone)

      What this fixes (the deployment pains of it1-it34):
        - No stale image PID holding a port after restart
        - No supervisor/child respawn race (EADDRINUSE)
        - No "boot-on-SSH" spawn that needs a trigger
        - No two DB roles to keep in sync
        - No first-run ceremony from the supervisor's
          auth-client handshake
    |)))
  )
  (subsection migration-plan
   (doc "Order of operations for the make pass")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      Phase 1 — make the image standalone:
        1. image/bin/unikernel.ml: drop mirage, plain unix
           main reading PP_* from env
        2. image/lib/boot.ml: drop manifest path, read env
        3. image/lib/dune + dune-project: drop mirage/unix
        4. Verify `dune build image' green; image runs alone

      Phase 2 — delete the other two packages:
        5. rm -r supervisor/ auth/ (+ dune stanzas)
        6. common/: trim auth_types.ml, protocol.ml
        7. Verify `dune build @all' green (lisp + common +
           image only)

      Phase 3 — deploy machinery:
        8. Add /etc/rc.d/pricklypear (single service)
        9. Update post-receive hook (build 3 packages,
           restart one service)
       10. nginx: drop /admin block; keep / proxy
       11. scripts/dev.sh: PG + image only

      Phase 4 — prod cutover:
       12. Push, deploy, start single service
       13. Delete supervisor + authd rc.d entries
       14. Verify /dabar /todos /notes /repl live

      Rollback (if needed): revert the commit; the old
      supervisor+authd binaries still exist in prior
      releases until pruned. No data migration needed —
      002_auth tables are additive and inert if unread.
    |)))
  )
 )
)